    {
      "kind": "serendipity",
      "subjectId": "gadss-adt-requirements",
      "note": "The GADSS one-minute distress-tracking date has moved twice and most secondary sources still print the original 2021 figure.",
      "why": "Anything quoting 2021 is repeating superseded trade-press coverage. As adopted the requirement applies from 1 January 2025 to aeroplanes over 27,000 kg whose certificate of airworthiness was first issued on or after 1 January 2024. This is a live example of a regulatory citation that decays, and an argument for recording a retrieval date on every regulatory claim.",
      "source": "https://aviationweek.com/mro/icao-postpones-distress-tracking-mandate"
    },
    {
      "kind": "serendipity",
      "subjectId": "turbine-engine-parameters",
      "note": "Gas generator speed is reported as a percentage of a reference speed, and 100% is about 37,500 rpm on a PT6A-27.",
      "why": "This is the single highest-value finding of the run for open-aviation-telemetry. The existing schema has engine.rpm with a 0-6000 range and an 1800-2700 airborne band. For a turbine that is not a units error — the modelled quantity does not exist, the real shaft speed is an order of magnitude outside the declared range, and the operationally limiting parameter (ITT) has no field. It is a schema change, not a constant change.",
      "source": "https://www.aviationpros.com/engines-components/article/10387616/troubleshooting-taking-a-look-at-the-pt6a27-engine"
    },
    {
      "kind": "serendipity",
      "subjectId": "adsb-position-integrity",
      "note": "Fleet-wide NACp degradation is a GNSS interference detector, not a fleet of broken transponders.",
      "why": "A tracking platform that stores the integrity categories gets regional GNSS interference monitoring for free — a genuinely useful product feature that falls out of not discarding fields. A platform that stores only latitude and longitude cannot ever add it retrospectively, because the data was never kept.",
      "source": "https://insidegnss.com/protecting-gnss-for-safe-aviation/"
    },
    {
      "kind": "serendipity",
      "subjectId": "iridium-sbd-constraints",
      "note": "SBD latency scales with message length: about 5 s at 70 bytes versus about 20 s at the 340-byte maximum.",
      "why": "Message size is not only a cost lever, it is a latency lever. That inverts the usual instinct to batch: for a tracking application, several small reports arrive sooner than one large one, which matters directly for how quickly a position is known.",
      "source": "https://metocean.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/DEV_Iridium-SBD_Best-Practices-Guide_NOV17.pdf"
    },
    {
      "kind": "gap-note",
      "subjectId": "polar-navigation",
      "note": "Specific ARINC 429 label numbers for magnetic versus true heading were not obtainable from public sources.",
      "why": "ARINC 429 Part 1 is paywalled and public summaries document the word structure without reproducing the full label table. The structural claim — that units, resolution and range are fixed per label, so magnetic and true heading are distinct labels — is sourced; the specific label numbers are not and were therefore not asserted.",
      "source": "https://www.aim-online.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/aim-tutorial-oview429-190712-u.pdf"
    },
    {
      "kind": "gap-note",
      "subjectId": "adsb-position-integrity",
      "note": "The DO-260B NIC/NACp/SIL value tables themselves were not retrievable; only their existence and location were confirmed.",
      "why": "RTCA DO-260B is a paid standard. Public sources confirm the tables are in §2.2.3 and describe what each category means, but do not reproduce the integer-to-containment-radius mapping. Any implementation needs the document; quoting specific radii from memory would be exactly the confabulation this rubric's source-required rule exists to prevent.",
      "source": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2023/10/17/2023-22710/inclusion-of-additional-automatic-dependent-surveillance-broadcast-ads-b-out-technical-standard"
    },
    {
      "kind": "spec-suggestion",
      "note": "Add a field for the cost of getting this wrong, distinct from severity.",
      "why": "Several subjects here are cheap to fix now and very expensive after data has accumulated — the turbine parameter set, storing both altitudes, storing integrity categories. Severity captures what breaks; it does not capture that the fix window closes. A `fix_window` field (before-schema / before-launch / anytime) would let a reader sort by urgency rather than by damage."
    },
    {
      "kind": "spec-suggestion",
      "note": "Several difficult cases are schema-shaped: the model cannot represent the truth, rather than computing it wrongly.",
      "why": "Altitude (two quantities, one field), heading (value without reference), engine parameters (wrong quantity entirely), identifiers (four ids, one key), timestamps (two times, one field). That recurrence is itself the finding: the dominant failure mode in telemetry modelling is not bad logic, it is a schema that cannot express the domain. Worth stating explicitly in the rendered output."
    }
  ]
